WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.178 --> 00:00:03.217
Cooper: No. Don't let me leave, Murph!

00:00:03.217 --> 00:00:04.050
Ah!

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.340
No! No! No! [sobbing]

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.050
Narrator: This is the
moment when the big twist

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.750
in "Interstellar" is revealed:

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.040
that Cooper was the ghost

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.580
interacting with Murph's bookshelf

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.480
from the beginning of the film.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.170
This is the moment we find out

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.940
the big twist in "The Prestige":

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.730
that Angier cloned and
killed himself every night

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.510
to achieve his magic trick.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:25.040
And this is the moment we find out

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.670
the big twist in "Following":

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.890
that Bill is being framed for murder.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.770
All these scenes have something in common,

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.250
and not just that they're
directed by Christopher Nolan.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.040
We see each of these scenes
multiple times in their movies.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.060
So, how are scenes we've already watched

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.880
still able to deliver such a big twist?

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.773
The secret lies in
Nolan's use of recurrence.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.200
Nolan uses the idea of
recurrence in his films

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.700
in a myriad of ways.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.700
In "Inception," recurrence is featured

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.600
as a main component of the plot,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.770
creating dreams within dreams.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.200
In "The Prestige," Nolan
explores recurrence as a motif.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.700
The film features twins and clones,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.491
diaries within diaries,

00:01:03.491 --> 00:01:05.300
and the main characters repeatedly

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.510
sabotaging each other's magic tricks.

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:08.530
And in "Memento,"

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.170
the very structure of
the film is recursive,

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.900
as the film constantly alternates

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.070
between scenes from the past
and scenes from the present.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:19.210
Put simply, recurrence
is continued repetition.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.270
However, one of Nolan's
greatest uses of recurrence

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:23.300
is how he employs it

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.980
to deliver twists at the end of his films.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.600
A simple first example to
help wrap your head around

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.520
how Nolan uses recurrence to
pull off his twist endings

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.260
comes from a scene in "The Prestige"

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.190
that Nolan replays three
times throughout the film.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.160
At 47 seconds into the film,

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.680
we see a bunch of top hats in a forest.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.670
When the scene cuts away from
the hats, it's confusing.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:44.920
We don't see the hats again

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.111
for another hour and 25 minutes,

00:01:47.111 --> 00:01:49.090
when we learn that the
hats have been duplicated

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.350
by Tesla's machine.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.980
This time, the scene
makes a little more sense.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.383
However, the real significance of the hats

00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:57.170
isn't revealed until Nolan shows us

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.694
the top hats once again

00:01:58.694 --> 00:02:00.336
right before his final reveal.

00:02:00.336 --> 00:02:01.920
Angier achieves his magic trick

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.560
by cloning himself every night

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.830
using the same Tesla machine
that duplicated the top hats,

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.400
ultimately drowning each of his clones.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.560
So we see the scene three times,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.910
and our experience of it the third time

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.730
is significantly different

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.600
than when we saw it the first time.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.170
Suddenly, the perplexing
scene from the opening

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.510
now makes sense because
of all of the context

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.120
that we get throughout the movie.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.870
And even though the movie presents us

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.423
with everything we need to figure out

00:02:27.423 --> 00:02:29.352
the significance of the opening image...

00:02:29.352 --> 00:02:31.470
Angier: Well, which one is mine?

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.350
Tesla: They're all you hats, Mr. Angier.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.270
Narrator: We don't tend to
apply what we've learned

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.230
until the scene plays again.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.820
Now, one could argue that the scene

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.460
is just a simple flashback,

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.280
but it's actually more than that.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.860
While many films and
filmmakers use flashbacks

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:46.350
and nonlinear storytelling,

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.180
what makes Nolan's style distinct

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.320
is that he doesn't flash
back just once in his films.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.630
Instead of structuring his nonlinear films

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:54.960
to have distinct sections

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.130
that are out of order
chronologically, like "Pulp Fiction,"

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:00.670
the nonlinear structures of Nolan's films

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.980
constantly flash back and forth
between distinct timelines,

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.400
adding more and more
context to each timeline

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:07.550
with each recurrence,

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.700
until, ultimately, everything makes sense.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.010
Now, using recurrence does not at all

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.860
make a film inherently better,

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.030
but it does happen to be an
extremely helpful technique

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.060
when trying to create a twist ending.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.150
Even among movies with twist endings,

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:23.480
Nolan's use of recurrence

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.610
sets his films apart from the rest.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.310
For example, in the film
"The Usual Suspects,"

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.760
there is certainly a twist
at the end of the film,

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.510
but it's a one-and-done reveal.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.370
The seemingly meek Verbal
was lying throughout the film

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.740
and is actually the fabled
crime boss Keyser Söze.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.040
So while it's an
entertaining twist ending,

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.500
the film purposefully
misleads the audience

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.210
throughout the majority of the film,

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.590
and there are very few
clues placed throughout

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:51.070
that lead the audience to the
truth about Verbal's identity.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.446
However, in Nolan's films,
the audience is almost always

00:03:54.446 --> 00:03:56.580
given the opportunity to solve the mystery

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.220
through recursively
presented clues and scenes

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.280
that are shown over and over again.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.000
Take, for example, the
opening shot of "Inception,"

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.270
where a haggard Cobb washes up on a beach

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.070
and talks with an old man.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:10.710
This scene is revisited
again at the end of the film,

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.980
now that the audience
understands the context

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:14.710
and realizes what's going on:

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:17.540
that this beach is limbo and
that Cobb has traveled there

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:20.000
in order to save Saito, the old man.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.330
Nolan even shows a very similar
scene of Cobb and Ariadne

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.540
washing up on the beaches of Cobb's limbo,

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.380
but he doesn't leave enough
time for the audience to process

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.380
the significance of
washing up on the beach

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.007
and its connection to the short scene

00:04:32.007 --> 00:04:34.030
that happened two hours before.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:36.270
Nolan is constantly
presenting the audience

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.870
with all of the answers
throughout his films,

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:41.200
but we just don't quite
know what to look for

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.540
until he shows us right at the very end.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.495
The fact that Nolan is able to repeatedly

00:04:45.495 --> 00:04:46.870
show the audience the truth

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:48.920
without them realizing what's happening

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.290
is what makes his films so iconic.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.400
While the twist of "The Usual Suspects"

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.590
is like telling you that the
duck you've been staring at

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.680
is actually a rabbit,

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.630
the twists in Nolan's films
are more like a hidden puzzle.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.910
There's a colorful image or a scene

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:05.510
that doesn't make sense when
you look at it the first time;

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.290
however, when Nolan finally
gives the audience the key

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.530
and you revisit the image,
you can see things clearly.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.570
This is why revisiting Nolan's
scenes are such a twist.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.440
The answer has been there the whole time,

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:17.920
hiding in plain sight.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.453
Even Nolan's films that don't have

00:05:19.453 --> 00:05:20.736
traditional twist endings

00:05:20.736 --> 00:05:22.220
have recursive elements.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:23.880
For example, in "Dunkirk,"

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.830
he shows the same scene from
several different perspectives,

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.850
which results in a miniature twist.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.440
While initially it appears
that the pilot, Collins,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.350
is fine after being shot
down by an enemy plane,

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.030
waving to the other pilot,

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.950
when Nolan later cuts to Collins'
perspective of the event,

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.940
it turns out that he's actually not OK.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.720
His waving is actually him
struggling to open his cockpit

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:45.660
while he slowly begins to drown.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.690
While this is one of
the most basic examples

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:48.810
of Nolan's recurrence,

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:52.140
it shows how such a simple
tool can be so powerful.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.170
Luckily, knowing how Nolan works

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:57.530
won't necessarily ruin your
experience watching his films.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.530
As Cutter famously says at
the end of "The Prestige"...

